### Tested versions

- Reproducible in 4.6.rc1 _(But from what I can see in the code should be reproducible in all versions of Godot 4 at least, haven't checked Godot 3)_

### System information

Godot v4.6.rc1 - Fedora Linux 42 (Workstation Edition) on Wayland - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 1 monitor - OpenGL 3 (Compatibility) - NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2070 (nvidia; 580.82.09) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-8700 CPU @ 3.20GHz (12 threads) - 62.70 GiB memory

### Issue description

Mipmap LOD bias settings don't do anything in Compatibility Mode. It can be set in project settings: `rendering/textures/default_filters/texture_mipmap_bias` or set directly via code changing the variable `texture_mipmap_bias` in the viewport or subviewport.

It does what it's supposed to do in Vulkan, it doesn't do anything in OpenGL (Compatibility) renderer. I couldn't find anywhere in the documentation documenting that it doesn't work on compability mode.

I'm running into a mipmap issue with WebXR (So I have to use opengl), and was trying to use this setting to fix it, then I noticed it wasn't doing anything.

```c++
void RenderSceneBuffersGLES3::configure(const RenderSceneBuffersConfiguration *p_config) {
	GLES3::TextureStorage *texture_storage = GLES3::TextureStorage::get_singleton();
	GLES3::Config *config = GLES3::Config::get_singleton();

	free_render_buffer_data();

	internal_size = p_config->get_internal_size();
	target_size = p_config->get_target_size();
	scaling_3d_mode = p_config->get_scaling_3d_mode();
	//fsr_sharpness = p_config->get_fsr_sharpness();
	//texture_mipmap_bias = p_config->get_texture_mipmap_bias();
	//anisotropic_filtering_level = p_config->get_anisotropic_filtering_level();
	render_target = p_config->get_render_target();
	msaa3d.mode = p_config->get_msaa_3d();
	//screen_space_aa = p_config->get_screen_space_aa();
	//use_debanding = p_config->get_use_debanding();
	view_count = config->multiview_supported ? p_config->get_view_count() : 1;
```

In the `drivers/gles3/storage/render_scene_buffers_gles3.cpp` file the texture_mipmap_bias appears but is commented out.

### Steps to reproduce

Easiest way is to use the minimal reproduction project below that allows you to quickly change the texture_mipmap_bias with the arrow keys on the keyboard and see the results.
- Open the MRP
- Run the project in Forward+, use the arrow keys to see the mipmap bias changes affecting the textures in the environment.
- Restart the project in Compatibility mode, use the arrow keys and see that nothing is happening.

### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)

[test_mipmap_lod_bias_multi_viewport.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/24642835/test_mipmap_lod_bias_multi_viewport.zip)

This minimal reprodution project comes from another issue that already has been solved that relates to vulkan ( #84083 ) but it's a perfect reproduction project to verify this quickly in compatibility mode.
